\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 190\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 11\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://sebastianraschka.com/llm-architecture-gallery/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388676","date_published":"2026-03-15T16:01:09Z","author":{"name":"tzury","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=tzury"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388646","title":"Ask HN: How is AI-assisted coding going for you professionally?","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eComment sections on AI threads tend to split into \"we're all cooked\" and \"AI is useless.\" I'd like to cut through the noise and learn what's actually working and what isn't, from concrete experience.\u003cp\u003eIf you've recently used AI tools for professional coding work, tell us about it.\u003cp\u003eWhat tools did you use? What worked well and why? What challenges did you hit, and how (if at all) did you solve them?\u003cp\u003ePlease share enough context (stack, project type, team size, experience level) for others to learn from your experience.\u003cp\u003eThe goal is to build a grounded picture of where AI-assisted development actually stands in March 2026, without the hot air.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388646\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388646\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 207\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 349\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388646","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388646","date_published":"2026-03-15T15:58:23Z","author":{"name":"svara","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=svara"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47388273","title":"Learning athletic humanoid tennis skills from imperfect human motion data","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://zzk273.github.io/LATENT/\"\u003ehttps://zzk273.github.io/LATENT/\